WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.180
In 2018, FOSTA CESTA moved sex ads offshore without cutting trafficking arrests. This is Unintended

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:13.380
Consequences, episode 176. On April 11, 2018, President Donald Trump signed the Allow

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:19.940
States and Victims to Fight Online Sex Trafficking Act, known as FOSTA CESTA. The measure amended

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:26.180
section 230 of the Communications Decency Act. It removed liability protections for platforms

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:29.360
that knowingly facilitated sex trafficking or prostitution.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.600
Commercial sex advertising moved on to overseas servers and into encrypted channels.

00:00:35.537 --> 00:00:40.077
Representative Anne Wagner of Missouri had introduced the Fight Online Sex Trafficking

00:00:40.077 --> 00:00:41.817
Act in the House in 2017.

00:00:42.737 --> 00:00:46.477
Senator Rob Portman of Ohio sponsored the Companion Bill in the Senate.

00:00:47.057 --> 00:00:49.217
The Stop Enabling Sex Traffickers Act.

00:00:49.697 --> 00:00:52.557
Both lawmakers had examined BackPage.com.

00:00:52.557 --> 00:00:57.637
them. They concluded that section 230 was shielding a site that profited from ads linked

00:00:57.637 --> 00:01:02.517
to trafficking. They argued that platforms should face federal and state criminal and civil

00:01:02.517 --> 00:01:08.557
liability when they knowingly promoted or facilitated prostitution. At the time, backpage

00:01:08.557 --> 00:01:13.997
generated substantial revenue from adult services sections. Federal investigators had already

00:01:13.997 --> 00:01:19.517
documented cases in which traffickers used the site to advertise minors. Wagner and Portman

00:01:19.517 --> 00:01:24.397
presented the bills as a targeted update. The change would encourage platforms to remove

00:01:24.397 --> 00:01:31.437
illegal content without broadly censoring speech. The package passed the House 388-25 on February

00:01:31.437 --> 00:01:40.237
27, 2018, and the Senate 97-2 on March 21, 2018. Supporters took those near unanimous votes as

00:01:40.237 --> 00:01:45.517
evidence of a clear gap in accountability. They saw the carve-out as restoring the original intent

00:01:45.517 --> 00:01:51.477
of Section 230, limited to knowing promotion of prostitution or trafficking, with the rest

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:56.833
of the immunity framework left intact. Craig's list removed its entire personal section

00:01:56.833 --> 00:02:04.253
on March 23, 2018, citing the pending legislation. The bill became law on April 11, just five days

00:02:04.253 --> 00:02:10.573
after federal authorities seized BackPage.com. Major United States platforms deleted or restricted

00:02:10.573 --> 00:02:15.393
adopted adult advertising categories to avoid new criminal exposure under Title 18 of

00:02:15.393 --> 00:02:22.593
the United States Code, Section 2421A. Proponents said prosecutors and victims could finally

00:02:22.593 --> 00:02:27.593
reach operators who had claimed immunity. Within weeks, activity that had sat on a few

00:02:27.593 --> 00:02:32.653
large domestic platforms began appearing on smaller sites hosted outside the United States.

00:02:33.173 --> 00:02:38.353
The new provision set penalties of up to 10 years for operating a platform to promote prostitution,

00:02:38.353 --> 00:02:44.873
and up to 25 years in aggravated cases involving five or more persons or reckless disregard of trafficking.

00:02:45.843 --> 00:03:00.003
A government accountability office report covering 2014 through 2020 found that platform operators relocated servers and domains to countries including the Netherlands, Turkey, and Germany to reduce exposure to United States jurisdiction.

00:03:00.003 --> 00:03:03.563
The market fragmented across many more foreign sites.

00:03:04.523 --> 00:03:08.243
Investigators needed a mutual legal assistance treaty for each new jurisdiction.

00:03:08.843 --> 00:03:14.523
A Carnegie Mellon University study, summarized by Legal Clarity in 2026, showed that the

00:03:14.523 --> 00:03:20.023
volume of online commercial sex ads dipped right after FOSTA CESTA, then returned to prior

00:03:20.023 --> 00:03:23.003
levels across a larger number of overseas platforms.

00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:24.943
Demand had not disappeared.

00:03:25.583 --> 00:03:27.203
New offshore sites filled the gap.

00:03:27.203 --> 00:03:32.003
The liability rule changed the location of the ads, not the underlying volume.

00:03:32.483 --> 00:03:35.263
The Hacking Hustling Participatory Research Project,

00:03:35.563 --> 00:03:38.703
published in 2020, surveyed online sex workers.

00:03:39.143 --> 00:03:44.263
72.5% reported increased economic instability after the law took effect.

00:03:44.703 --> 00:03:48.423
33.8% reported more violent encounters with clients

00:03:48.423 --> 00:03:51.083
once screening tools on domestic sites disappeared.

00:03:51.743 --> 00:03:54.463
Platforms could no longer safely host screening information

00:03:54.463 --> 00:03:56.963
or review boards without risking liability.

00:03:57.643 --> 00:04:02.463
Many workers moved to encrypted messaging apps and offshore boards with fewer safety features

00:04:02.463 --> 00:04:06.023
and lost the client histories that had previously reduced risk.

00:04:06.503 --> 00:04:11.163
The government accountability office noted that only one of the 11 federal criminal cases

00:04:11.163 --> 00:04:14.743
brought against platform operators between 2014 and 2020

00:04:15.403 --> 00:04:17.643
actually relied on the new foster provisions.

00:04:18.203 --> 00:04:22.003
A 2021 article in porn studies citing those findings

00:04:22.003 --> 00:04:26.023
programs, described how cross-border evidence requests lengthened investigations.

00:04:26.423 --> 00:04:31.363
What had been a single point request became a series of separate treaty processes, each

00:04:31.363 --> 00:04:33.363
with its own delays and disclosure limits.

00:04:34.063 --> 00:04:36.583
Agency's faced a larger set of smaller targets.

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:41.623
Fresh evidence collection across borders stretched limited resources and reduced the number

00:04:41.623 --> 00:04:43.963
of cases that could be finished in the same period.

00:04:44.503 --> 00:04:50.243
The shift also reduced the visibility of transactions to United States researchers and service organizations

00:04:50.243 --> 00:04:52.683
operations that had monitored domestic platforms.

00:04:53.163 --> 00:04:58.103
Operators had calculated that moving hosting overseas lowered the probability of United States

00:04:58.103 --> 00:05:03.423
criminal charges more than any incremental moderation would, because the statute exposed

00:05:03.423 --> 00:05:05.983
them to federal prosecution and state level suits.

00:05:07.054 --> 00:05:12.294
Federal prosecutors have continued to rely primarily on pre-existing statutes rather than the

00:05:12.294 --> 00:05:15.574
new FOSTA tools according to the government accountability office.

00:05:15.574 --> 00:05:20.574
No civil damages or restitution orders have been issued under the law's private right

00:05:20.574 --> 00:05:21.114
of action.

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:26.314
Sex worker organizations and civil liberties groups have called for repeal or amendment,

00:05:26.874 --> 00:05:31.414
arguing that the liability change produced measurable safety harms without corresponding

00:05:31.414 --> 00:05:32.634
reductions in trafficking.

00:05:33.274 --> 00:05:37.894
A Taylor and Francis analysis linked a further pattern to longer enforcement delays.

00:05:38.494 --> 00:05:42.974
Some platforms kept a minimal United States presence while routing traffic through foreign

00:05:42.974 --> 00:05:43.414
entities.

00:05:43.414 --> 00:05:47.834
companies. Researchers still track the offshore distribution first measured in the Government

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:53.594
Accountability Office report. The relocation incentive has not reversed, and law enforcement

00:05:53.594 --> 00:05:59.224
has fewer centralized points of contact. Strip immunity from one category of content,

00:05:59.624 --> 00:06:04.544
and an operator who can leave will price staying against leaving. If the duty bites only

00:06:04.544 --> 00:06:09.344
where the server sits, geography becomes the cheaper form of compliance, and moderation

00:06:09.344 --> 00:06:14.964
never has to win that comparison. Anyone drafting a platform liability statute can ask,

00:06:15.264 --> 00:06:19.644
before the shift, whether the new duty reaches only domestically-hosted services,

00:06:20.204 --> 00:06:24.384
and whether investigators already have the treaties and the staff for a market that splits.

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:29.944
A vote can look like accountability while the measurable work moves into legal systems the statute

00:06:29.944 --> 00:06:35.424
does not control. Those same incentives sit inside today's arguments over encryption and over

00:06:35.424 --> 00:06:40.624
content moderation rules, wherever a duty is costly only inside one country's servers

00:06:40.624 --> 00:06:43.164
or only on platforms that keep readable records.

00:06:43.824 --> 00:06:48.404
How might similar geographic incentives appear in today's debates over encryption or content

00:06:48.404 --> 00:06:49.384
moderation rules?

00:06:49.864 --> 00:06:51.104
That's the case for today.

00:06:51.704 --> 00:06:55.644
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd

00:06:55.644 --> 00:06:58.324
find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

